# Nexus OS — gaming image
#
# A bootable container. `bootc` turns this into a system: the image is
# the source of truth, updates replace it, and the previous image stays
# on disk so the bootloader can go back.
#
# Build it in stages. Each FROM below is a checkpoint you can build and
# boot on its own, so a failure tells you which layer broke rather than
# just that something did.

# Which Fedora this is built on.
#
# Declared once, before the first FROM, so the two stages that start
# from a Fedora image cannot drift apart. A base version named in two
# places is two places that have to agree, and they eventually do not.
#
# 42 was used until September 2026 and reached end of life on 27 May
# 2026. That is not a detail: an image-mode system's whole security
# story is that updates replace it, and there is nothing left to
# update from. It also cannot be made a Fedora Remix, because the
# generic-logos package below is not built for 42.
#
# The rule this encodes: a release ships on a Fedora that is still
# supported on the day it ships, and moving to the next one is
# ordinary maintenance rather than an emergency. Fedora supports a
# release until four weeks after the one two ahead of it, so there is
# always a supported version to move to and roughly a year to do it.
#
# Overridable, for trying the next one before committing to it:
#
#     podman build --build-arg FEDORA_VERSION=45 ...
ARG FEDORA_VERSION=44

# ---------------------------------------------------------- layer order
# Why the stages below are arranged the way they are.
#
# An image is a stack of layers, and changing one invalidates every
# layer above it. Nexus used to go in near the bottom and the desktop
# -- five hundred packages, most of the image -- on top of it. So any
# change to Nexus, down to the version number in os-release, rebuilt
# the whole desktop, and pushing it meant uploading gigabytes again.
# The first push to GHCR, on 22-23 September, took all night.
#
# That cost does not stay with whoever publishes. bootc upgrade pulls
# the layers that changed, so every installed machine would have
# downloaded a whole desktop to receive a one-line fix.
#
# So the order is now: Fedora, then what rarely changes (tools, logos,
# kernel arguments), then the packages, and Nexus last. A change to
# Nexus is a few megabytes, for the person pushing and for every
# machine pulling.
#
# The trade-off, stated rather than discovered: a cached package layer
# is a stale one. Rebuilding for a Nexus change reuses the packages
# from the last time they were installed, so Fedora's security updates
# reach an image only when the package layer is deliberately rebuilt:
#
#     podman pull quay.io/fedora/fedora-bootc:44
#     podman build --build-arg REFRESH="$(date +%Y%m%d)" ...
#
# The pull fetches a newer fedora-bootc if there is one; REFRESH
# forces the package installs to run again. The pull is its own
# command rather than --pull=newer because that spelling only exists
# from podman 4, and Pop!_OS 22.04 ships 3.4, where --pull takes only
# true or false and rejects "newer" outright. That is a big push, and it
# is the one that carries security fixes -- see image/README.md.

# Declared once, before the first FROM, for the same reason
# FEDORA_VERSION is: a stage that needs one restates the name without
# a value and inherits this.
#
# NEXUS_VERSION has no default on purpose. The version lives in one
# place -- project(VERSION ...) in CMakeLists.txt -- and the image asks
# the binary it just built (image/identity.sh). It used to live in
# three: CMakeLists for the binary, a default here for os-release, and
# another in publish.sh for the tag. On 23 September a laptop upgraded
# to an image whose os-release said 0.1.1 while `nexus --version` in
# the same image said 0.1.0, because the build argument reached one of
# the three and not the others.
#
# Passing --build-arg NEXUS_VERSION is still allowed, as a check: the
# build fails if it disagrees with the source, rather than one of the
# two quietly winning.
ARG NEXUS_VERSION
ARG NEXUS_PROFILE=minimalism

# ------------------------------------------------------------- builder
# Compiling happens here and stays here. The shipped image gets the
# binary and nothing else: no compiler, no source, no build tree.
#
# This also sidesteps a real problem. Deleting a COPYed directory
# inside a RUN fails with an I/O error under rootless podman's overlay
# filesystem -- so the answer is not to delete it, but never to put it
# in the shipped layer.
FROM quay.io/fedora/fedora-bootc:${FEDORA_VERSION} AS builder

RUN dnf install -y \
        cmake \
        gcc-c++ \
        make \
    && dnf clean all

COPY . /src

# The commit, passed in rather than read here: .containerignore keeps
# .git out of the build context, so the tree inside this stage has no
# repository to ask. Left unset, the binary reports an unknown commit
# and `nexus doctor` says so -- which is honest, but useless on an
# installed machine trying to identify itself.
ARG NEXUS_COMMIT=unknown

# NEXUS_BUILD_TESTS=OFF: the image needs the tool, not the test suite.
RUN cmake -S /src -B /build \
        -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
        -DNEXUS_BUILD_TESTS=OFF \
        -DNEXUS_COMMIT_OVERRIDE="${NEXUS_COMMIT}" \
    && cmake --build /build --parallel

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- os
# Fedora plus the things that almost never change. Not a target:
# nothing ships from here directly.

FROM quay.io/fedora/fedora-bootc:${FEDORA_VERSION} AS os

# Changing this reruns every install below -- the package refresh
# described above. Its value is only ever printed; it exists to be
# different from last time.
ARG REFRESH=none

# Nexus reads repository metadata, which arrives compressed in
# whichever format the tool that wrote it preferred. Fedora uses zstd
# for small repositories and zchunk for large ones, so an image that
# ships Nexus has to ship the decompressors or it can only read the
# small ones.
RUN echo "Package refresh: ${REFRESH}" \
    && dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        --setopt=install_weak_deps=False \
        zchunk \
        zstd \
    && dnf clean all

# Fedora's artwork out, generic artwork in.
#
# Fedora's trademark guidelines let anyone build on Fedora and say so,
# but not ship Fedora's marks. fedora-logos is where those marks live
# on a running system: the boot splash, the login screen, the icon
# every desktop shows for "about this computer". Keeping it while
# calling the result Nexus-CORE is using somebody else's trademark to
# label a different product -- which matters more, not less, once a
# release is sold.
#
# generic-logos is the package Fedora provides for exactly this. Both
# provide system-logos, so the swap is one package for another rather
# than a hole where the artwork was.
#
# Not available for Fedora 42, which is one of the reasons the base
# above moved.
#
# The rpm -q is the point of the whole block. dnf can report success
# on a transaction that resolved differently than intended, and an
# image that quietly kept Fedora's logos would look exactly like one
# that did not. This asks the machine what it actually has, and fails
# the build if the answer is wrong.
RUN set -eu; \
    if rpm -q fedora-logos > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        dnf swap -y fedora-logos generic-logos; \
    else \
        echo "fedora-logos was not installed; installing generic-logos"; \
        dnf install -y generic-logos; \
    fi; \
    if rpm -q fedora-logos > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo "fedora-logos is still installed; refusing to ship it." >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    rpm -q generic-logos; \
    dnf clean all

# Let somebody mistype the disk passphrase.
#
# systemd-cryptsetup gives up after three attempts. On a machine
# encrypted by default that is the most likely thing a real user gets
# wrong, and giving up does not look like giving up: the boot carries
# on for about five minutes emitting dracut warnings about a device
# that is never going to appear, ends in "Could not boot", and drops
# to an emergency shell whose own advice -- press Ctrl-D -- starts a
# job that waits forever for the same device.
#
# tries=0 means keep asking. There is nothing to protect by refusing:
# the disk is the thing guarding itself, and an attacker with the
# machine can reboot as many times as they like. The limit only ever
# punishes the owner.
#
# In kargs.d rather than passed to the installer, so it is a property
# of the image: it arrives on machines installed from this ISO and on
# machines that upgrade into it, without anyone having to remember.
#
# Verified 22 September 2026: in a VM, three wrong passphrases were
# followed by a fourth prompt, and /proc/cmdline carried the option.
RUN mkdir -p /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d \
    && printf '%s\n' \
        '# Keep asking for the disk passphrase instead of giving up' \
        '# after three attempts. See image/Containerfile.' \
        'kargs = ["rd.luks.options=tries=0"]' \
        > /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d/10-nexus-luks.toml

# ----------------------------------------------------- desktop-packages
# The desktop, before Nexus. Not a target on its own; `desktop` below
# is this plus Nexus.
#
# KDE on Wayland. Chosen over GNOME because gaming benefits from
# variable refresh and per-display scaling, which KDE has handled
# better for longer.
#
# --exclude=rootfiles: it ships /root/.bash_logout, and in a bootc
# image /root is managed by ostree rather than being an ordinary
# directory. RPM cannot unpack into it, and one failed unpack aborts
# the entire transaction.
#
# --setopt=install_weak_deps=False: Recommends are what somebody else
# thought you might like. An image should contain what it needs.
#
# The desktop comes from the profile, not from this file, and which
# profile is a build argument rather than a decision baked in here.
#
#     podman build --build-arg NEXUS_PROFILE=minimal ...
#
# One pipeline, several machines. That is the claim the whole design
# rests on, and hardcoding one profile here would leave it untested.
#
# The lists are generated by image/generate-lists.sh and committed:
# a build you cannot read is a build you cannot audit, and every line
# of those files says which capability it answers. Installing them
# does not need the nexus binary -- the list is already written --
# which is what lets this stage sit below Nexus.

FROM os AS desktop-packages

ARG NEXUS_PROFILE

# Kept rather than deleted, in /usr/share so the built image carries
# the list it was built from. That is worth a few kilobytes: an image
# that cannot say what it is made of is one nobody can check.
#
# Deleting it is also not possible. Removing a COPYed directory inside
# a RUN fails with an I/O error under rootless podman's overlay
# filesystem -- the same failure that killed the first build of the
# base stage, reintroduced here and caught the same way.
COPY image/generated /usr/share/nexus/lists

RUN set -eu; \
    list="/usr/share/nexus/lists/${NEXUS_PROFILE}.rpm.list"; \
    if [ ! -f "$list" ]; then \
        echo "No generated list for profile '${NEXUS_PROFILE}'." >&2; \
        echo "Have:" >&2; \
        ls /usr/share/nexus/lists >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    packages="$(grep -v '^#' "$list" | awk 'NF {print $1}')"; \
    echo "Installing ${NEXUS_PROFILE}:"; \
    echo "$packages"; \
    dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        --setopt=install_weak_deps=False \
        $packages; \
    dnf clean all

# Whether this machine boots to a desktop depends on whether the
# profile asked for one.
#
# minimal has a session and deliberately no display manager: you log
# in on a console and start it yourself. Enabling sddm here assumed
# every profile wanted the same machine, which is the assumption the
# build argument exists to remove.
RUN set -eu; \
    manager=""; \
    for candidate in sddm gdm lightdm; do \
        if systemctl list-unit-files "${candidate}.service" \
               --no-legend 2>/dev/null | grep -q .; then \
            manager="${candidate}"; \
            break; \
        fi; \
    done; \
    if [ -n "$manager" ]; then \
        echo "Display manager: ${manager}"; \
        systemctl enable "${manager}.service"; \
        systemctl set-default graphical.target; \
    else \
        echo "No display manager installed; booting to a console."; \
        systemctl set-default multi-user.target; \
    fi

# Fedora's artwork must still be gone.
#
# The os stage swapped it out, but that proves nothing about this
# one: a desktop is hundreds of packages, and any of them may require
# fedora-logos by name rather than the system-logos both packages
# provide. dnf would install it without comment, and the image would
# ship Fedora's marks again with the earlier check still reading as
# passed -- a check that looked at the base and said nothing about
# the machine.
#
# If this fails, the message names the package that wanted it, which
# is the thing to fix.
RUN if rpm -q fedora-logos > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo "fedora-logos came back in the desktop stage." >&2; \
        echo "Required by:" >&2; \
        rpm -q --whatrequires fedora-logos >&2 || true; \
        exit 1; \
    fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Nexus
# The same few layers end both `base` and `desktop` below: the binary,
# first boot, profiles, and what the system says it is. They are
# written out twice because a Containerfile has no way to include one
# block in two stages; image/identity.sh holds the logic, so what is
# repeated is only which files go where. Keep the two copies the same.
#
# The first-boot unit is in every target rather than only the last
# one: a --target build stops wherever it is told, and the unit once
# simply did not exist in the desktop image, which looked exactly like
# systemd refusing to run it.
#
# Every target ends by reinstalling the SELinux policy. dnf leaves the
# policy store inconsistent during a container build -- a module listed
# with its files missing -- and nothing notices until a deployment is
# finalized on a real machine, where refreshing the policy fails and
# takes the deployment with it. Reinstalling puts the files back;
# `semodule -B` cannot, because it renames directories and overlayfs
# cannot rename across layers. (The empty extra_varrun module the build
# also leaves is removed at first boot, where the filesystem allows it.)

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- base
# Fedora, the tools and Nexus, with no desktop. What
# image/generate-lists.sh runs `nexus image` inside.

FROM os AS base

ARG NEXUS_VERSION
ARG NEXUS_PROFILE

COPY --from=builder /build/cli/nexus /usr/bin/nexus
COPY image/first-boot/nexus-first-boot /usr/bin/nexus-first-boot
COPY image/first-boot/nexus-first-boot.service \
     /usr/lib/systemd/system/nexus-first-boot.service
COPY components/profiles /usr/share/nexus/profiles
COPY components/aliases /usr/share/nexus/aliases
COPY image/identity.sh /usr/libexec/nexus/identity

RUN NEXUS_VERSION="${NEXUS_VERSION}" NEXUS_PROFILE="${NEXUS_PROFILE}" \
    sh /usr/libexec/nexus/identity

ENV NEXUS_PROFILES=/usr/share/nexus/profiles
ENV NEXUS_ALIASES=/usr/share/nexus/aliases

RUN dnf reinstall -y selinux-policy-targeted && dnf clean all

# -------------------------------------------------------------- desktop
# What ships: the desktop packages, then Nexus on top.

FROM desktop-packages AS desktop

ARG NEXUS_VERSION
ARG NEXUS_PROFILE

COPY --from=builder /build/cli/nexus /usr/bin/nexus
COPY image/first-boot/nexus-first-boot /usr/bin/nexus-first-boot
COPY image/first-boot/nexus-first-boot.service \
     /usr/lib/systemd/system/nexus-first-boot.service
COPY components/profiles /usr/share/nexus/profiles
COPY components/aliases /usr/share/nexus/aliases
COPY image/identity.sh /usr/libexec/nexus/identity

RUN NEXUS_VERSION="${NEXUS_VERSION}" NEXUS_PROFILE="${NEXUS_PROFILE}" \
    sh /usr/libexec/nexus/identity

ENV NEXUS_PROFILES=/usr/share/nexus/profiles
ENV NEXUS_ALIASES=/usr/share/nexus/aliases

RUN dnf reinstall -y selinux-policy-targeted && dnf clean all

# Not what ships. It still stacks its packages above Nexus, so a
# gaming image pays the old cost on every change; worth the same
# reordering if it is ever released.
FROM desktop AS gaming

# Fedora bootc ships no 32-bit multilib. Steam needs it, so it is added
# explicitly rather than assumed -- see decisions 4 and 5.
RUN dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        mesa-dri-drivers.i686 \
        mesa-vulkan-drivers.i686 \
        mesa-libGL.i686 \
        mesa-libEGL.i686 \
        glibc.i686 \
        libgcc.i686 \
        alsa-lib.i686 \
        pulseaudio-libs.i686 \
        libX11.i686 \
        libXext.i686 \
        libXinerama.i686 \
        libXrandr.i686 \
        libXScrnSaver.i686 \
        nss.i686 \
    && dnf clean all

# RPM Fusion carries Steam. Enabling a third-party repository is a
# decision, not a detail: it is the only non-Fedora source in the
# image, and it is here because Steam is not in Fedora proper.
RUN dnf install -y \
        "https://mirrors.rpmfusion.org/free/fedora/rpmfusion-free-release-$(rpm -E %fedora).noarch.rpm" \
        "https://mirrors.rpmfusion.org/nonfree/fedora/rpmfusion-nonfree-release-$(rpm -E %fedora).noarch.rpm" \
    && dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        steam \
        gamemode \
        gamescope \
        mangohud \
        vulkan-tools \
    && dnf clean all

# Controllers. A gamepad that does not work is a bug a beginner cannot
# describe, so the drivers and udev rules are present from first boot.
RUN dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        kernel-modules-extra \
        libratbag-ratbagd \
    && dnf clean all

# Audio: PipeWire is already default on Fedora. Named here so the image
# states it rather than inheriting it silently.
RUN dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        pipewire \
        pipewire-pulseaudio \
        wireplumber \
    && dnf clean all

# --------------------------------------------------------------- nexus
# Rebuild the SELinux policy store.
#
# dnf writes into the store during a container build and what comes
# out is inconsistent: a module listed with its files missing. Nothing
# notices until a deployment is finalized on a real machine, where the
# policy is refreshed, fails, and takes the deployment with it.
#
# At the end of every stage a build can stop at, not once at the
# start: --target desktop never reaches the later stages, and a store
# rebuilt before six more installs is a store rebuilt too early.
# Remove a policy module that has no files.
#
# A container build leaves extra_varrun registered in the store as an
# empty directory. semodule cannot read past it -- not to rebuild the
# policy, not even to list modules -- so finalizing a deployment on a
# real machine fails and the deployment is discarded. That is what
# stopped rpm-ostree from layering a package, and it looked like
# every other kind of problem first.
#
# A module with no files provides no policy, so removing it loses
# nothing. With it gone, semodule reads 429 modules and layering
# works: packages survive a reboot and the machine stays atomic.
# (removed at first boot instead -- see first-boot/nexus-first-boot:
#  overlayfs refuses to delete it during a build, and it deletes
#  cleanly on a real filesystem.)

# Restore the SELinux policy files dnf left incomplete.
#
# A container build can leave a policy module listed with its files
# missing -- extra_varrun, in this case. Nothing notices until a
# deployment is finalized on a real machine, where refreshing the
# policy fails and takes the deployment with it, which is what
# stopped rpm-ostree from layering a package.
#
# Reinstalling puts the files back. Rebuilding the store with
# semodule -B would be the direct fix and cannot work here: it swaps
# directories by renaming them, and overlayfs cannot rename across
# layers.
RUN dnf reinstall -y selinux-policy-targeted && dnf clean all

FROM gaming AS final

LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="Nexus-CORE"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.description="A Linux system that explains its own decisions"
LABEL containers.bootc="1"

# Nothing is installed in this stage, so there is no policy store to
# repair here: the gaming stage above ends with the reinstall, and
# this stage adds only labels. Three more reinstalls used to sit
# below, each rebuilding a store that nothing had touched and each
# adding a layer worth a couple of hundred megabytes.
#
# They arrived by the same route as the duplicated comment blocks
# above them -- a patch script that checked whether its work was done
# by looking for a line that was already there, decided it was not,
# and appended a second copy. Three times.

# And once more at the very end, for the stages after desktop: the
# gaming stage adds RPM Fusion, a second set of packages that knows
# nothing about this decision.
RUN if rpm -q fedora-logos > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo "fedora-logos came back after the desktop stage." >&2; \
        rpm -q --whatrequires fedora-logos >&2 || true; \
        exit 1; \
    fi

# Last, so that it lints the image that ships.
#
# bootc requires this to succeed for the image to be considered
# valid, and an instruction after it is an instruction it never saw.
RUN bootc container lint
